---
/**
 * Original BotPlanet explainer — what actually holds a window robot on glass.
 *
 * WHY A DRAWING BEATS THE PARAGRAPH IT REPLACES. The guide has to answer one
 * question before any other: what happens when the power goes off. The answer
 * has three parts that are hard to hold in a sentence — a vacuum seal against
 * the glass, a battery that keeps the fan turning through a cut, and a safety
 * line clipped to something indoors — and a reader who does not see them
 * together tends to assume the tether is doing the holding. It is not. It is
 * the last of three defences, and the picture makes that ordering obvious.
 *
 * Conceptual illustration. No product is depicted, no mechanism is attributed
 * to a manufacturer, and no runtime figure appears — the battery-backup
 * duration varies by model and belongs on a review with its source.
 */
---
<figure class="dgm">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 640 320" width="100%" role="img"
    aria-label="Cross-section of a window-cleaning robot on glass: a vacuum seal presses it to the pane, an internal battery keeps the fan running if mains power is cut, and a safety line is anchored indoors as the last defence.">
    <rect x="0" y="0" width="640" height="320" rx="14" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--border)"/>

    <text x="28" y="38" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#f5f5f7">Three things stop it falling</text>
    <text x="28" y="58" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">In this order. The line is the last one, not the first.</text>

    <!-- the glass, seen edge-on -->
    <rect x="286" y="82" width="10" height="206" rx="2" fill="rgba(124,193,255,0.22)" stroke="#4a90d9"/>
    <text x="252" y="304" font-size="11" fill="#8b8b95" text-anchor="middle">glass</text>
    <text x="150" y="80" font-size="11" fill="#8b8b95">outside</text>
    <text x="470" y="80" font-size="11" fill="#8b8b95">inside</text>

    <!-- the robot, clamped to the outside face -->
    <rect x="212" y="150" width="74" height="70" rx="10" fill="#2b3242" stroke="#7c93ff" stroke-width="2"/>
    <circle cx="249" cy="185" r="15" fill="none" stroke="#7c93ff" stroke-width="2"/>
    <text x="249" y="190" font-size="11" fill="#7c93ff" text-anchor="middle">fan</text>

    <!-- 1. suction arrows into the glass -->
    <g>
      <path d="M196 168 L210 172" stroke="#4fd1c5" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#a)"/>
      <path d="M196 202 L210 198" stroke="#4fd1c5" stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#a)"/>
      {/* THE NUMBER GOES BESIDE ITS OWN HEADING. Floated out into the margin,
          badge 2 landed on the last line of block 1's body text and badge 3 sat
          nowhere near the words it numbered — an ordering device that made the
          order harder to read. */}
      <circle cx="37" cy="181" r="9" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
      <text x="37" y="185" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0b0f17" text-anchor="middle">1</text>
      <text x="52" y="186" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#4fd1c5">Vacuum seal</text>
      <text x="28" y="204" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">The fan pulls air out from</text>
      <text x="28" y="219" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">under the body. Air pressure</text>
      <text x="28" y="234" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">outside does the holding.</text>
    </g>

    <!-- 2. battery backup -->
    <g>
      <rect x="228" y="122" width="42" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="#f0b429"/>
      <rect x="230" y="124" width="26" height="12" rx="3" fill="#f0b429"/>
      <circle cx="37" cy="265" r="9" fill="#f0b429"/>
      <text x="37" y="269" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0b0f17" text-anchor="middle">2</text>
      <text x="52" y="270" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#f0b429">Battery backup</text>
      <text x="28" y="288" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">Keeps the fan turning through</text>
      <text x="28" y="303" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">a power cut, not for a whole clean.</text>
    </g>

    <!-- 3. safety line, anchored indoors -->
    <g>
      <path d="M280 150 C 340 120, 420 130, 470 160" fill="none" stroke="#ff8f6b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
      <rect x="466" y="158" width="30" height="30" rx="6" fill="#2b3242" stroke="#ff8f6b" stroke-width="2"/>
      <text x="481" y="178" font-size="11" fill="#ff8f6b" text-anchor="middle">⚓</text>
      <circle cx="521" cy="211" r="9" fill="#ff8f6b"/>
      <text x="521" y="215" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0b0f17" text-anchor="middle">3</text>
      <text x="536" y="216" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#ff8f6b">Safety line</text>
      <text x="512" y="234" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">Clipped to something</text>
      <text x="512" y="249" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">solid indoors. It catches</text>
      <text x="512" y="264" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">the robot; it does not</text>
      <text x="512" y="279" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">hold it up.</text>
    </g>

    <defs>
      <marker id="a" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="6" refY="3.5" orient="auto">
        <path d="M0 0 L7 3.5 L0 7 Z" fill="#4fd1c5"/>
      </marker>
    </defs>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="note">
    The seal does the work, the battery buys time, and the line is what stops a falling robot
    reaching the pavement. A machine that skips any of the three is doing something the other two
    have to cover.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .dgm { margin: 1.75rem 0; }
  .dgm svg { max-width: 640px; display: block; }
  .dgm .note { margin-top: 8px; }
</style>
